Yes 👍 FreeRTOS must be included (built and flashed) into the device firmware. But we don’t “install” it like software on a laptop. Instead, it becomes part of the firmware that runs on the microcontroller.

How FreeRTOS Gets Onto the Device

For a device using FreeRTOS, the typical workflow is:

Developer downloads FreeRTOS source code

Write application code (sensor reading, MQTT communication, etc.)

Compile everything together (FreeRTOS + your code)

Flash the compiled firmware to the microcontroller

Developer Code
      +
FreeRTOS Kernel
      +
Drivers / Libraries
      ↓
Compiled Firmware (.bin / .hex)
      ↓
Flash to IoT Device

So FreeRTOS becomes part of the device firmware.

Example With an IoT Device

Imagine an ESP32 IoT sensor.

Firmware structure might look like:

Firmware
 ├── Bootloader
 ├── FreeRTOS Kernel
 ├── WiFi Driver
 ├── MQTT Client
 └── Application Tasks

When the device powers on:

Bootloader starts

FreeRTOS kernel loads

FreeRTOS starts running tasks

Example FreeRTOS Task Code

Example tasks running on the device:

void sensorTask(void *params)
{
    while(1)
    {
        read_sensor();
        vTaskDelay(1000);
    }
}

void mqttTask(void *params)
{
    while(1)
    {
        send_data_to_cloud();
        vTaskDelay(2000);
    }
}

FreeRTOS schedules these tasks.

When Using AWS IoT

If the device connects to AWS IoT Core, the firmware may also include:

MQTT library

TLS security

OTA update library

Device shadow library

These help the device communicate with AWS.

Simple Way to Think About It
Laptop → Install Windows/Linux
IoT Device → Flash Firmware (with FreeRTOS inside)

So:

PCs install OS

Microcontrollers flash firmware that contains FreeRTOS

✅ Short answer:
Yes, FreeRTOS runs on the device, but it is compiled into the firmware and flashed to the microcontroller, not installed like normal software.
